What I do, for what its worth, is have a directory with
a long list of possible nethackrc files, each with a specific
name and matching class/race type.  I then run a script that
randomly selects a character file from this directory, copies
it to my ~/.nethackrc file and starts nethack (well, slashem
actually).

This gives me a character name with customized attributes including
pet names etc.

The catch is that even having 40 or 50 characters to select
from can be limiting.

I have, in the past, tried to do things like use the -s option
to extract scores, parse the results and weight the probability
of each character based on the inverse of their high score...
this made me have more chance to play a character I had not
done well at, as a forced learning exercise.  But recently I've
gone back to an equal weighting.


